      "notes": "All claims are well-supported by their cited evidence. Tier assignments are correct: baseline seat/vote statistics (E2, E11) are measurable facts from 2024 election data; projections about STV effects are correctly labelled projected. The E2 source is consoc.org.uk (media/think-tank) but it cites House of Commons Library data, which is an acceptable relay. Key advocacy sources (Electoral Reform Society, Make Votes Matter) are used only for projected claims, not as sole basis for the direction verdict. The direction 'improves' and magnitude 'major' are proportionate: the structural democratic-equality gains (vote-seat proportionality, wasted votes) are well-documented and the mechanism is not speculative. The counter-claim on constituency-link dilution is included as a projected claim (E13), giving honest balance without false equivalence. No lever leakage \u2014 the verdict correctly scores the democratic-rights dimension of STV rather than judging the policy as a political lever. No party identity language in the rationale. The magnitude 'major' is defensible given the structural nature of the reform and the scale of documented distortion (30-point seat/vote gap). One minor note: E10 ('expected to increase voter engagement and turnout') is cited in the evidence but not used as a claim in the verdict, which is appropriate given its speculative nature. Overall the verdict is sound for publication."
    },
